  "query_preview": "(Today through Thursday)...\nIssued at 232 AM EDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nSurface analysis early this morning shows weak high pressure in \nplace over Indiana and Ohio...drifting slowly east. Low pressure was \nfound over SE NB, and a shield of clouds ahead of this low was seen \non GOES19 pushing toward Central Indiana. Aloft, water vapor imagery \nshowed a moderate trough over the northern plains states, while \nridging was found from the middle Mississippi Valley north to the \nCentral Great Lakes. Water vapor imagery showed plentiful mid and \nupper moisture streaming north across Indiana under the ridge. Dew \npoints across Central Indiana were in the 60s.\n\nToday...\n\nThe surface high pressure system and associated ridging aloft is \nexpected to slowly drift east through the day while the surface low \nand associated upper trough to the west advance eastward also. The \ncloud shield found over MO and IL is expected to arrive through the \nday. Forecast soundings show a top down saturation occurring through \nthe day as these systems advance toward our area. Furthermore, CU \ndevelopment is expected as convective temperatures are reached. Thus \nincreasing clouds are expected along with high temperatures near \npersistence. \n\nHRRR shows the arrival of scattered showers with embedded \nthunderstorms arriving in western Central Indiana late this \nafternoon and early this evening. THus will begin to add some pops \nto the western parts of the forecast area late this afternoon.\n\nTonight through Sunday...\n\nPeriodic rain showers with embedded thunderstorms are expected \ntonight through much of the weekend. Models depict the moderate \ntrough to the northwest evolving into an upper low arriving across \nCentral Indiana and eventually being cut-off from the flow aloft on \nSaturday and Sunday, stalling over Indiana. This will keep cyclonic \nflow in place across Indiana on Saturday and Sunday, with plentiful \nmoisture and moderate forcing.  Forecast soundings through Sunday \nshow a mostly saturated column with this forcing in place, along \nwith pwats over 1.8 inches. Instability looks conditional and a few \nembedded thunderstorms cannot be ruled out. Given the expected \nshowery nature of the precipitation spinning around the upper low, \nshowers and and storms look to be sporadic during the weekend rather \nthan just a constant rain. Overall though, confidence is high for \nrain through the weekend and heavy rains cannot be ruled out given \nthe conditional convection and high pwats. Look for cooler highs in \nmid to upper 70s on Saturday and Sunday, with lows in the upper 60s.\n\nMonday through Thursday....\n\nRain chances will continue to persist on Monday in to Tuesday as the \ndeparting upper trough over KY, cut off from strong surface flow \naloft, will continue to linger in our vicinity. Models suggest \ncontinued wrap around moisture and cyclonic lower level flow that \nwill impact southeast parts of Central Indiana. Meanwhile stronger \nsurface high pressure will be moving across the Great Lakes along \nwith ridging aloft. This will allow for better chances for dry and \ncooler weather across northeastern Central Indiana, amid cool \nnortheasterly flow. This should result in at or below normal \ntemperatures on Monday and Tuesday.\n\nBetter chances for dry and warmer weather will return on Wednesday \nand Thursday as the upper flow looks to become a stronger across the \nnorthern CONUS, helping to push the lingering upper trough east. \nbroad surface high pressure looks to be in place beneath this flow \naloft and gulf flow appears limited. Air mass modification should \nallow high to at or above seasonal normals here.",
